{"name":"Mallard Blue","hex":"#3f6378","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mallard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Anas platyrhynchos, a common and widespread dabbling duck, natively found throughout the Northern Hemisphere, whose male has a distinctive dark green head.","The meat of this bird, eaten as food."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mallard"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mallard","blocks":[{"text":"The mallard or wild duck is a dabbling duck that breeds throughout temperate and subtropical North America, Eurasia, and North Africa. It has been introduced to New Zealand, Australia, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Argentina, Chile, Colombia, the Falkland Islands, and South Africa. Belonging to the subfamily Anatinae of the waterfowl family Anatidae, mallards live in wetlands, eat water plants and small animals, and are social animals preferring to congregate in groups or flocks of varying sizes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Anas_platyrhynchos_male_female_quadrat.jpg/330px-Anas_platyrhynchos_male_female_quadrat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mallard","wikidata":"Q25348"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/AnasPlatyrhynchosIUCN2019_2.png/500px-AnasPlatyrhynchosIUCN2019_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/AnasPlatyrhynchosIUCN2019_2.png/1280px-AnasPlatyrhynchosIUCN2019_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/AnasPlatyrhynchosIUCN2019_2.png/500px-AnasPlatyrhynchosIUCN2019_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnasPlatyrhynchosIUCN2019_2.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Anas_platyrhynchos_conboschas%2C_Sisimiut%2C_Greenland_1.jpg/500px-Anas_platyrhynchos_conboschas%2C_Sisimiut%2C_Greenland_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Anas_platyrhynchos_conboschas%2C_Sisimiut%2C_Greenland_1.jpg/1280px-Anas_platyrhynchos_conboschas%2C_Sisimiut%2C_Greenland_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Anas_platyrhynchos_conboschas%2C_Sisimiut%2C_Greenland_1.jpg/500px-